"FAQ (https://pixabay.com/service/faq/)\nciteturn154view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://pixabay.com/service/faq/\",\"pattern\":\"adding it to a book\"}); Total lines: 379\nL0:   * Explore\nL1:     * 【0†Log in】 【1†Join】\nL2: \nL3: Media【2†Photos】【3†Illustrations】【4†Vectors】【5†Videos】【6†Music】【7†Sound Effects】【8†3D Models】【9†GIFs】\nL4: \nL5: * * *\nL6: \nL7: Discover【10†Curated Collections】【11†Pixabay Radio】【12†Popular Images】【13†Popular Videos】【14†Popular Music】【15†Popular Searches】\nL8: \nL9: Trending【16†Halloween】【17†Diwali】【18†Fall】【19†Wallpaper】【20†Nature】\nL10: \nL11: * * *\nL12: \nL13: Community【21†Contests】【22†Creators】【23†Ambassadors】【24†Forum】【25†Blog】\nL14: About【26†About Us】【27†FAQ】【28†License Summary】【29†Terms of Service】【30†Privacy Policy】【31†Cookies Policy】【32†Digital Services Act】【33†Report Content】【34†API】 Language\nL15: \nL16: 【35†Čeština】【36†Dansk】【37†Deutsch】【27†English】【38†Español】【39†Français】【40†Indonesia】【41†Italiano】【42†Magyar】【43†Nederlands】【44†Norsk】【45†Polski】【46†Português】【47†Română】【48†Slovenčina】【49†Suomi】【50†Svenska】【51†Türkçe】【52†Việt】【53†ไทย】【54†Български】【55†Русский】【56†Ελληνική】【57†日本語】【58†한국어】【59†简体中文】\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19:   * 【0†Log in】\nL20:   * 【1†Join】\nL21:   * 【60†Upload】\nL22:   * 【2†Photos】 【3†Illustrations】 【4†Vectors】 【5†Videos】 【6†Music】 【7†Sound Effects】 【8†3D Models】 【9†GIFs】\nL23: \nL24: ✖\nL25: \nL26: Images\nL27: \nL28: Images Photos Vector graphics Illustrations Videos Music Sound Effects 3D Models GIFs Users\nL29: \nL30: * * *\nL31: \nL32: 【61†Search options →】\nL33: \nL34: [Input][Input][Input: Search]\nL35: \nL36: [Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input]\nL37: ### Pixabay\nL38: \nL39: 【26†About Us】\nL40: \nL41: 【27†FAQ】\nL42: \nL43: 【28†License Summary】\nL44: \nL45: 【29†Terms of Service】\nL46: \nL47: 【30†Privacy Policy】\nL48: \nL49: 【31†Cookies Policy】\nL50: \nL51: 【32†Digital Services Act】\nL52: \nL53: 【33†Report content】\nL54: \nL55: 【24†Forum】\nL56: \nL57: # FAQ\nL58: ## Get Started\nL59: \nL60: What is Pixabay?\nL61: \nL62: Pixabay is a vibrant community of creatives, sharing royalty-free HD photos and stock images, new illustration vectors, high-quality videos, popular animated GIFs, 3D models and best audio music that are free to download. All contents are released under the Pixabay License, which makes them safe to use without attribution - even for commercial purposes.\nL63: \nL64: How can I upload photos, videos, music, illustrations, GIFs, 3D models and other media on Pixabay?\nL65: To upload photos, videos, music, illustrations, GIFs, 3D models, sound-effects and vectors, you need to be a registered user on Pixabay first. If you are not yet registered, you can 【62†sign up】 here. Once you have signed up you can upload any media by clicking the \"upload” button in the top right corner of the page.\nL66: \nL67: Can I use your photos, videos, music, illustrations, GIFs and other media?\nL68: You can copy, modify, distribute, and use Pixabay's royalty-free stock photos, illustrations, videos, music, GIFs, 3D models, vectors and sound effects, even for commercial purposes. However, depicted content may still be protected by trademarks, publicity or privacy rights. 【63†Learn more about the Pixabay license.】\nL69: \nL70: Are all photos, videos, music, illustrations, GIFs and other media on Pixabay free to use?\nL71: Pixabay photos, illustrations, videos, music, animated GIFs, 3D models, vectors and sound effects are free to use for commercial and non-commercial purposes. However, depicted content may still be protected by trademarks, publicity or privacy rights. While no payment is needed to use the media, it is always appreciated when you credit the contributor as it provides exposure to their work and encourages them to continue sharing. While not required, we also allow users to make donations to contributors.\nL72: Find out how, under 'Downloads and Donations'.\nL73: How can I download images, videos, music, and other media from Pixabay?\nL74: \nL75: To download any media (stock photos, illustrations, videos, music, animated GIFs, 3D models, vectors and sound effects), go to the specific media page of the content that you want to download, then click the ‘Free Download' button, alternately select the down arrow to choose a size to download.\nL76: \nL77: \nL78: \nL79: Why is the legal entity Canva Germany and not Pixabay?\nL80: Canva purchased Pixabay back in 2019. Since then, we've continued to operate as Pixabay and will continue to do so. This entity change is an organizational and administrative change only. It does not impact our brand or community.\nL81: ## License & Terms\nL82: \nL83: If I do wish to use media on a product like those mentioned above, how much do I need to alter it?\nL84: \nL85: The golden rule of thumb is to consider whether the product you are creating effectively amounts to reselling the original image and whether a reasonable person on the street would look side-by-side at the original image and your product and consider them to be identical (or effectively identical).\nL86: If you've made some creative effort to incorporate a photo into a broader design, this should generally be sufficient (see examples in the next question below). Applying a filter over a photo, minor cropping, or simply changing some colours will generally not be enough.\nL87: \nL88: \nL89: \nL90: What are examples of significantly altered media?\nL91: \nL92: - Adding the photo to a video montage.\nL93: \nL94: - Incorporation into a greater work (e.g. adding it to a book, app, game).\nL95: - Adding the photo to any work where the reason someone would buy it is because of what you have made, rather than the image.\nL96: \nL97: \nL98: \nL99: Can I sell Pixabay media on canvas or wallpaper?\nL100: \nL101: Digitally printing images onto a canvas or wallpaper for sale is not allowed if there were no significant changes made to the image(s).\nL102: \nL103: \nL104: \nL105: Can I use Pixabay photos and images for personal purposes?\nL106: \nL107: Yes, you can use the images in any way you want for personal use.\nL108: Can I use media to print on things (e.g. phone covers, mugs, shirts, pillows) and sell them?\nL109: \nL110: Yes – but only if they are significantly altered, as mentioned above.\nL111: \nL112: \nL113: \nL114: Can I use Pixabay images as a logo for my brand (and potentially sell merchandise with this logo)?\nL115: \nL116: Yes, you can use Pixabay images for your logo. However, you cannot claim ownership of any images on Pixabay, which means you cannot register your logo as a trademark. Kindly check our 【29†Terms of Service】 for further information.\nL117: I want to use an image which features a famous brand, logo or trademark and sell it afterwards. Is that allowed?\nL118: \nL119: We suggest removing things like logos, trademarks or any other features that may identify a particular brand, as those items are likely to be trademarked or protected in some way, and reproduction of those marks (especially in a commercial way) may result in legal implications.\nL120: \nL121: \nL122: \nL123: Can I use Pixabay images for my book cover?\nL124: Yes, in this case you are incorporating the media into a greater work. People would buy the book for the book itself (reading it) and not for the media you are using.\nL125: \nL126: \nL127: \nL128: Can I use Pixabay images of people and use them for commercial purposes?\nL129: \nL130: It is best to contact the artist and inquire about a model release if a person is recognisable.\nL131: Please note that the patent or trademark rights of any person are not affected by our license, so we encourage our users to always check whether they have all rights to use such content.\nL132: \nL133: \nL134: \nL135: How is my content being used for AI & machine learning?\nL136: The general license applied to the content you upload to Pixabay means we're able to use this content for the development of responsible AI models and the creation of exciting new features. We believe that everything at Pixabay starts with our incredible community of contributors, and we want to ensure transparency around how your content is used in addition to the ability to opt-out of using your content for AI and machine learning.\nL137: By allowing us to use your content for AI and ML training, your content helps others by\nL138: \nL139: - Responsible AI model training: By allowing us to use your content, you help improve responsible AI models. This contributes to a future where the leading AI models are the responsibly and ethically trained ones, made possible by contributors like you.\nL140: - Detecting AI-generated content: In addition to generative AI, we are working on systems that can detect if something is AI-generated or not to ensure authenticity as this technology continues to evolve. This requires a large & diverse set of non-AI and AI content to compare against.\nL141: - Improving diversity, representation, and safety: Your content helps contribute to AI models being more inclusive and representative of different cultures, races, and genders. Additionally, your participation helps in creating a safer community where we can detect and remove prohibited or illegal content.\nL142: If you would prefer we don't use your content for future training of our AI models, you can opt out by updating your preferences in your Account settings by clicking 【64†here】 and toggle off the \"AI training” section.\nL143: ## Upload Guidelines\nL144: \nL145: Can I upload fan art or adaptations of someone else's creative work?\nL146: \nL147: No. Uploading fan art or altered versions of existing content (like movie characters, celebrity images, or well-known games) is not allowed unless the original is in the public domain or you have clear written permission from the copyright holder.\nL148: \nL149: Learn more: 【65†Intellectual Property Explained】\nL150: \nL151: Can I upload content that includes brand names or logos?\nL152: Be careful with content that features trademarks—like company logos, product packaging, or brand slogans. Even if these appear in your photos, illustrations, or AI-generated content, uploading them may lead to legal issues or removal.\nL153: \nL154: Learn more about trademarks and platform safety: 【65†Intellectual Property Explained】\nL155: \nL156: What are the rules for uploading AI-generated content?\nL157: AI content is welcome on Pixabay, but it must be original, clearly labeled, and free from copyrighted styles, trademarks, or depictions of real people. You must check the \"AI-label” toggle when uploading and follow the guidelines to ensure it doesn't mimic or reuse protected work.\nL158: \nL159: Read our full guide on AI and copyright: 【65†Intellectual Property Explained】\nL160: \nL161: What media uploads are not allowed on Pixabay?\nL162: You are not allowed to upload media that have signatures, watermarks or are advertisements. This can be considered spamming and might lead to the closure of your account. Please check out our 【66†Acceptable Use Policy】 for more information.\nL163: \nL164: I am a creator. Can I earn money through Pixabay?\nL165: Pixabay is a free photo, illustration, video, GIF, 3D model and music sharing website powered by our generous community of photographers, videographers, illustrators, GIF creators, 3D designers and musicians. While not required, users can make donations to artists to thank them for their contributions. Find out how under 'Downloads & Donations'.\nL166: \nL167: Why was my upload not accepted?\nL168: \nL169: Uploads are declined if they do not meet our quality guidelines, learn more 【67†here】.\nL170: My upload was accepted but doesn't show up on my profile. Why?\nL171: \nL172: It usually takes a few minutes for new media to show up in your profile. In rare cases, it may take a few days. If many uploads are missing, please check our forum (【24†https://pixabay.com/forum/】) for any incidents or contact us.\nL173: \nL174: What do the labels \"Approved\" and \"Published\" mean?\nL175: An upload that is marked as \"Published\" only appears in your profile.\nL176: An upload that is marked as \"Approved\" appears on your profile and additionally in the Pixabay search results.\nL177: \nL178: Is there a length limit for tracks uploaded to Pixabay?\nL179: \nL180: Audio can be uploaded in the file formats of either MP3, WAV, AAC,FLAC, AIF and M4A up to 100MB and < 15 minutes.\nL181: \nL182: My music play numbers are not updating on my audio tracks. Why?\nL183: We update play numbers once a day, so it will take up to 24 hours for you to see changes. Please create a post 【68†here 】if you are still having issues after 24 hours.\nL184: ## Downloads & Donations\nL185: \nL186: Can I use Pixabay photos, videos, music, illustrations, GIFs, 3D models and other media on Facebook and other social platforms?\nL187: \nL188: Yes, you can use Pixabay (stock photos, illustrations, videos, music, animated GIFs, 3D models, vectors and sound effects) on social media platforms.\nL189: \nL190: How can I donate to a contributor on Pixabay?\nL191: You can donate by clicking the \"Donate” button right after downloading any kind of media. Alternatively, you can donate by going to the user's profile and by clicking the green ‘Donate' button found alongside the ‘Message' & ‘More options' buttons on the top right part of their profile page.\nL192: \nL193: How can I receive donations?\nL194: Currently, artists can only receive donations through PayPal. Please indicate your PayPal email address or Merchant ID through the settings page. The Merchant ID is a 13 digit code, which can be found on your PayPal account's settings page.\nL195: \nL196: Can I make donations without PayPal?\nL197: \nL198: We only accept PayPal for donations to users.\nL199: ## Account & Settings\nL200: \nL201: How can I delete photos, videos, music, illustrations, GIFs, 3D models and other media uploaded on Pixabay?\nL202: \nL203: You can delete your upload(s) on the My Media page.  Just click your avatar photo on the top right of the menu then click 'My Media' in the dropdown. Hover over the content and click on the pencil icon that appears > Select ‘Settings' > ‘Delete media'.\nL204: Please note, if your Content has any downloads, your content will be deleted from search results, but the URLs will continue to live on our website archivally as a reference for the benefit of Pixabay community members who have already downloaded your Content.  This is because when you upload Content to Pixabay you agree to the broad Pixabay license in our Pixabay 【29†Terms of Service】, which means that you grant an irrevocable, perpetual, worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty free license to your Content, and warrant that you have obtained all necessary approvals and that your Content does not infringe any third party rights.  This allows us to offer our vibrant Pixabay community a way to download royalty-free content for their commercial or non-commercial use, and reference back to where that content came from if they need to in future.\nL205: If you're having any trouble with deleting your uploads, please 【69†contact us】 and we'd be happy to help once we have verified your account.\nL206: \nL207: How do I delete my Pixabay account?\nL208: \nL209: A deactivation link for this purpose is given on the 【64†settings page 】of your account.\nL210: ## API, Partnerships & Trademark Policy\nL211: \nL212: Does Pixabay offer an API?\nL213: \nL214: Yes, we do have an API. Learn all about it in the 【34†API documentation】.\nL215: \nL216: Where do I find Pixabay's trademark policy?\nL217: \nL218: Anyone who uses Canva's trademarks must comply with Canva's Trademark Policy. This Policy sets out Canva's requirements around referring to or using Canva's trademarks. 【70†Find more info here†www.canva.com】.\nL219: ## Community\nL220: \nL221: Where do I find Pixabay's upload and quality guidelines?\nL222: \nL223: You can find all of our media guidelines 【71†in our blog】.\nL224: \nL225: How do I set up my profile on Pixabay?\nL226: \nL227: Your profile allows you to create your own space on Pixabay. It will represent your portfolio and whatever you want to share with the community. 【72†Learn more about how to get the most out of your profile.】\nL228: \nL229: How do I get support?\nL230: \nL231: You can either 【73†ask your question on the forum】 or 【69†contact us】 directly.\nL232: Can anyone post on the forum?\nL233: \nL234: Yes, anyone can contribute to the 【24†Pixabay forum here】. Please check out our 【74†forum rules and communication guidelines】.\nL235: ## Music\nL236: \nL237: Can Pixabay beats and/or instrumentals be used in original vocal compositions that are then distributed on Digital Service Providers (DSPs)?\nL238: \nL239: Under the Pixabay Content License, you can't sell or distribute content (either in digital or physical form) on a standalone basis (i.e. where no creative effort has been applied to the content and it remains substantially the same form as it exists on the Pixabay website).\nL240: So long as you've created a unique, original creative work, the Pixabay license does allow use for both non-commercial and commercial purposes.\nL241: However, distributing music via DSPs (such as Spotify, Apple Music, or similar platforms) may involve additional platform or distributor requirements. It is the user's responsibility to ensure that their final work does not improperly claim, restrict, or monetise the original Pixabay track, and that any third-party permissions or licenses required by a distributor are satisfied.\nL242: Attribution or revenue sharing isn't required, but you're welcome to credit the original creator if you'd like. Suggested format: \"by [Contributor] via 【75†Pixabay】\".\nL243: \nL244: You can read more about the license here: 【28†Pixabay Content License Summary】\nL245: \nL246: Can I use Pixabay music in videos I sell or distribute?\nL247: Yes. You may use Pixabay music in commercial video projects, including content you sell or distribute (such as client videos, promotional materials, or paid training content), as long as the music is part of a larger creative work and not distributed as a standalone file.\nL248: \nL249: Can I publish content using Pixabay music on Over the Top (OTT) or subscription platforms?\nL250: Yes. Music from Pixabay can be used in content published on OTT or subscription platforms (e.g. documentaries, short films, branded content), provided the music is embedded as part of a creative project and not distributed separately or claimed as your own.\nL251: \nL252: Can I use Pixabay music in podcasts or audiobooks?\nL253: \nL254: Yes. Pixabay music can be used as background or intro/outro music in podcasts and audiobooks, provided the music is not offered on its own or in a way that mimics a music distribution service.\nL255: What is Content ID?\nL256: \nL257: Content ID is YouTube's automated copyright detection system. When a video is uploaded, YouTube scans it against a database of registered music, videos, and other media. If a match is found, the system may place a Content ID claim on the video based on the rights holder's preferences.\nL258: Even though music on Pixabay is free to use under the Pixabay Content License, some contributors or their distributors may choose to register their tracks with Content ID. This can result in automated claims even when the track is used legally.\nL259: \nL260: Is a Content ID claim the same as a copyright strike?\nL261: \nL262: No. A Content ID claim is not a copyright strike.\nL263: \nL264: - Content ID Claim: May affect monetisation, visibility, or regional availability. It does not affect your channel standing.\nL265: - Copyright Strike: A formal takedown request from a rights holder that does affect your channel. Three strikes can lead to account termination.\nL266: \nL267: Why did I get a claim if I used music from Pixabay?\nL268: \nL269: Some contributors upload their tracks to Pixabay for free use but also register them with Content ID (directly or via a distributor). As a result, Content ID may flag your video even if you used the track legally under the Pixabay license.\nL270: \nL271: Why do contributors register their music with Content ID?\nL272: Contributors may register their music with Content ID to:\nL273: \nL274: - Monitor how and where their music is used on YouTube\nL275: \nL276: - Prevent unauthorized re-uploads or misuse\nL277: \nL278: - Monetise their work on other platforms while still offering it freely on Pixabay\nL279: \nL280: This helps creators protect their work, but it can result in automated claims even for properly licensed use.\nL281: \nL282: What if I used music from Canva?\nL283: Canva integrates audio tracks from Pixabay into its media library. If you used music from Canva that originated on Pixabay, it is still covered by the Pixabay Content License.\nL284: \nL285: However, the same rules apply: if the contributor has registered their track with Content ID, your video may still trigger a claim. You can dispute the claim using the same steps outlined below, using your Canva project or download information as supporting documentation.\nL286: \nL287: How do I remove a Content ID claim on YouTube?\nL288: 1. Go to YouTube Studio → Content\nL289: \nL290: 2. Find the video with the claim and click \"See Details\"\nL291: \nL292: 3. Click \"Dispute\" and choose \"I have permission or a license to use the content\"\nL293: \nL294: 4. In the message box, include:\nL295: \nL296: - The track title\nL297: \nL298: - The track URL on Pixabay\nL299: \nL300: - A link to the Pixabay license: 【28†https://pixabay.com/service/license-summary/ 】\nL301: \nL302: - A short explanation of how the music is used in your video\nL303: \nL304: You can also follow 【76†this】 guide for step-by-step help.\nL305: \nL306: What if my dispute is rejected?\nL307: You may submit a second-level appeal, but please note:\nL308: \nL309: - If the rights holder rejects the appeal again, it could escalate to a copyright strike\nL310: \nL311: - Only proceed if you're confident your use complies with the Pixabay Content License\nL312: \nL313: How can I avoid future claims?\nL314: \nL315: - Keep records of your downloads (file name, screenshots, or project files)\nL316: \nL317: - Only download music directly from the official Pixabay site\nL318: - Avoid uploading music-only content (e.g. static images with just an audio track), which is more likely to be flagged\nL319: \nL320: - If you want to reduce the likelihood of automated claims, you may prefer tracks not registered with Content ID\nL321: \nL322: - Tracks displaying a shield icon are registered with Content ID and may trigger claims, which can usually be disputed using the Download certificate\nL323: \nL324: What about other platforms like Instagram, Facebook, or Pinterest?\nL325: These platforms also use automated detection systems that may mute or block content. If this happens:\nL326: \nL327: - Dispute using the same information you would provide for YouTube\nL328: \nL329: - Include the track link and Pixabay license summary\nL330: \nL331: - If needed, direct the platform to contact info@pixabay.com for verification\nL332: \nL333: Can I get a license certificate for all tracks?\nL334: License certificates are available for tracks registered with YouTube's Content ID. For other tracks, users should keep the download link, file name, and the Pixabay Content License summary as proof of permitted use.\nL335: \nL336: Why did I receive a Pixabay duplicate/copyright notice after my track was reviewed on Pixabay, even though it's original?\nL337: This may occur due to a mismatch in our detection system or because accompanying assets (such as album art or thumbnails) include copyrighted elements. For example, using images of well-known people, characters, or brands without rights may trigger a flag even if the audio itself is original.\nL338: \nL339: Can I use Pixabay music with AI tools (for example, to add AI-generated lyrics or vocals)?\nL340: Yes. Using AI tools to add lyrics, vocals, or otherwise adapt Pixabay music is allowed as long as the final result is a new, original creative work and the music is not redistributed on a standalone basis. Using AI does not change the Pixabay license rules. However, users are responsible for ensuring their final work complies with platform and distributor requirements and does not improperly claim or restrict the original Pixabay track.\nL341: ## Technical Questions\nL342: \nL343: How much content can I upload?\nL344: \nL345: New contributors will start with an upload limit of 60 files. Consistent uploads of good or great quality content will boost your upload limit by 10 files, on a rolling basis.\nL346: \nL347: 【77†Find more info here.】\nL348: \nL349: How can I avoid seeing adult content?\nL350: Whilst you won't find any strong violence and/or highly explicit content on Pixabay, we do accept nude photography and other visual content that might be considered offensive to some users. In order to filter out these search results, you can make use of our SafeSearch filter. 【78†Learn more here.】\nL351: ## Content Moderation\nL352: \nL353: How do we moderate content?\nL354: \nL355: Pixabay employs a blend of automated tools and human moderators to identify and address content or behavior that violates our 【29†Terms of Service】. This includes media, user comments, and forum discussions. Content that has been reported to us, or flagged as potentially violative by our automated systems, is reviewed by our team.\nL356: \nL357: What enforcement actions do we take?\nL358: Upon detecting a violation, we take various actions, typically notifying the content owner via email. These actions may include:\nL359: \nL360: - Content removal: Offending media is removed from the platform.\nL361: \nL362: - Account suspension: Access to Pixabay may be temporarily or permanently suspended.\nL363: \nL364: - Visibility restriction: Content may only be visible when Safe Search mode is deactivated.\nL365: Accounts primarily used to violate our Terms of Service or perpetuate serious harms are immediately permanently suspended. Repeated violations over time may also result in permanent suspension.\nL366: \nL367: How do I report content?\nL368: To report content or behaviour on Pixabay that violates our 【66†Acceptable Use Policy】 or 【29†Terms of Service】, hover over the media and click the flag icon (desktop), or select (i) > 'Report' (mobile). You can also use 【33†this】 form. For full reporting guidance, including how to report illegal content under EU or UK law, see our 【79†Content Safety & Compliance】 page.\nL369: \nL370: How do I appeal a content moderation decision?\nL371: \nL372: If you disagree with a content moderation decision, you can appeal within 180 days.\nL373: To appeal a decision about content, go to your '【80†My media】' section, select the relevant content and use the 'Lodge an appeal' function. Please include your reason for appealing, and any information or evidence that supports your appeal.\nL374: \nL375: If your account has been suspended, please email info@pixabay.com with a reference to your account, and your reason for appealing.\nL376: We'll review your appeal and notify you of the outcome. Successful appeals may result in content or account restoration. For full details on the appeals process and outcomes, see our 【79†Content Safety & Compliance】 page.\nL377: \nL378: ▲\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFAQ (https://pixabay.com/service/faq/)\nciteturn154view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://pixabay.com/service/faq/\",\"pattern\":\"Audio Content\"}); Total lines: 379\nNo matching text found for \"Audio Content\""
